ords can not <ei86 distract> the world from the facts of Iraqi aggression . " An international land , sea and air force has <ei87 mobilized> since Iraq 's <ei88 invasion> , which was <ei89 sparked> by <ei90 disputes> over oil , land and repayment of war loans . In the largest U.S. military <ei91 operation> since Vietnam , an estimated 20,000 American GIs have already <ei92 massed> to <ei93 defend> Saudi Arabia . " We do n't just <ei94 arrive> , " <ei95 said> four-star Gen. John Dailey , assistant commandant of the U.S. Marine Corps . " We 're there to <ei96 stay> for a fairly lengthy period . " Egypt , Syria , Morocco and Bangladesh also <ei98 committed> ground troops , to a much lesser degree . The U.S. Navy <ei99 has> 27 ships in the maritime barricade of Iraq . They are <ei100 aided> by Britain , West Germany , Australia , Canada , the Netherlands and Belgium . Bush was <ei101 expected> to <ei102 authorize> naval commanders to <ei103 use> " the minimum force necessary " to <ei104 interdict> shipments to and from Iraq , a U.S. official <ei105 said> . That could include <ei106 firing> across the bow to <ei107 halt> a ship . In the air , U.S. Air Force fliers <ei108 say> they have <ei109 engaged> in " a little cat and mouse " with Iraqi warplanes , which have <ei110 retreated> when weapons radar <ei111 locks> onto them . " They do n't <ei112 want> to <ei113 play> with us , " one U.S. crew chief <ei114 said> . In Kuwait , the Iraqis have <ei115 rimmed> the capital city with an air-defense system , <ei116 according> to a U.S. official who spoke on the condition of anonymity . He <ei117 declined> to <ei118 say> if the weapons <ei119 included> missiles , but the Iraqis have them in their arsenal . The Iraqis also possess chemical weapons . The combined <ei120 operations> are designed to <ei121 isolate> and <ei122 strangle> Iraq until it <ei123 retreats> from Kuwait . The <ei124 quarantine> <ei125 hopes> to <ei126 staunch> the <ei127 flow> of Iraqi oil , which is Iraq 's