 U.S. official <ei45 told> The Associated Press . Iraq <ei46 said> the <ei47 roundup> was to <ei48 protect> them from unspecified <ei49 threats> ; British Foreign Office minister William Waldegrave <ei50 called> the order " grave and sinister . " " What we <ei51 fear> is that they will be <ei52 interned> somewhere , most likely in Iraq , " Waldegrave <ei53 said> . A total of about 3,000 Americans , 3,000 Britons and more than 450 Japanese are in Iraq and Kuwait . Overall , more than 2 million foreigners are in both countries . Iraq has called them " restrictees . " In addition to the estimated 45,000 Marines to ultimately be part of Operation Desert Shield , Stealth fighter planes and the aircraft carrier John F. Kennedy are also <ei54 headed> to Saudi Arabia to <ei55 protect> it from Iraqi expansionism . In Washington , Pentagon spokesman Pete Williams <ei56 said> Iraq has <ei57 continued> to <ei58 increase> its armed forces in Kuwait and they now number about 160,000 . Saddam has been under international quarantine since his Aug . 2 <ei61 power-grab> , or what he <ei62 calls> an " eternal <ei63 merger> " with Kuwait . In a long verbal <ei64 attack> read on Iraqi television Thursday , Saddam repeatedly <ei66 called> Bush " a liar " and <ei67 said> a shooting <ei68 war> could <ei69 produce> body bags courtesy of Baghdad . " We <ei70 continue> to <ei71 pray> and <ei72 pray> hard to God so that there will be no <ei74 confrontation> whereby you will <ei75 receive> thousands of Americans wrapped in sad coffins after you had <ei76 pushed> them into a dark tunnel , " Saddam <ei77 said> . He <ei78 called> U.S. soldiers <ei79 massing> in Saudi Arabia the real occupiers in the Persian Gulf . <ei80 Replied> State Department deputy spokesman Richard Boucher , " We have n't really <ei81 analyzed> the statement in detail but it <ei82 appears> to be just another example of his outlandish rhetoric and his <ei83 attempts> to <ei84 distort> the truth . " We <ei85 believe> that his words can not <ei86 distract> the world from the facts of Iraqi aggression . " An international land , sea and air force has <ei87 mobilized> since Iraq 's <ei88 invasion> , which was <ei89 sparked> by <ei90 disputes> over oil , land and repayment of war loans . In the largest U.S. military <ei91 operation> since Vietnam , an estimated 20,000 American GIs have already <ei92 massed> to <ei93 defend> Saudi Arabia . " We do n't just <ei94 arrive> , " <ei95 said> four-star Gen. John Dailey , assistant commandant of the U.S. Marine Corps . " We 're there to <ei96 stay> for a fairly lengthy period . " Egypt , Syria , Morocco and Bangladesh also <ei98 committed> ground troops , to a much lesser degree . The U.S. Navy <ei99 has> 27 ships in the maritime barricade of Iraq . They are <ei100 aided> by Britain , West Germany , Australia , Canada , the Netherlands and Belgium . Bush was <ei101 expected> to <ei102 authorize> naval commanders to <ei103 use> " the minimum force necessary " to <ei104 interdict> shipments to and from Iraq , a U.S. official <ei105 said> . That could include <ei106 firing> across the bow to <ei107 halt> a ship . In the air , U.S. Air Force fliers <ei108 say> they have <ei109 engaged> in " a little cat and mouse " with Iraqi warplanes , which have <ei110 retreated> when weapons radar <ei111 locks> onto them . " They do n't <ei112 want> to <ei113 play> with us , " one U.S. crew chief <ei114 said> . In Kuwait , the Iraqis have <ei115 rimmed> the capital city with an air-defense system , <ei116 according> to a U.S. official who spoke on the condition of anonymity . He <ei117 declined> to <ei118 say> if the weapons <ei119 included> missiles , but the Iraqis have them in their arsenal . The Iraqis also possess chemical weapons . The combined <ei120 operations> are designed to <ei121 isolate> and <ei122 strangle> Iraq until it <ei123 retreats> from Kuwait . The <ei124 quarantine> <ei125 hopes> to <ei126 staunch> the <ei127 flow> of Iraqi oil , which is Iraq 's economic lifeblood , and <ei128 clamp> down on food and supplies <ei129 going> in. Iraq now <ei131 controls> 20 percent of the world 's oil reserves with its <ei133 conquest> of Kuwait . Only Saudi Arabia <ei134 has> more oil reserves . The economic <ei135 chokehold> appears to be working . The Lloyd 's List International newspaper , which monitors worldwide shipping , <ei136 said> Iraq 's fleet of 80 tankers and cargo ships has <ei137 stopped> regular <ei138 trading> . John Prescott , a shipping correspondent , <ei139 said> there was no <ei140 shipping> in Kuwaiti or Iraqi ports and that activity was <ei141 trailing> off in the Jordanian port of Aqaba . Bush 's chief objective in his <ei142 meeting> with Hussein was to <ei143 press> the king to <ei144 shut> down Iraq 's food and oil supply route from Aqaba on the Red Sea . Aqaba is Iraq 's only <ei145 outlet> now that an international noose has <ei147 tightened> . Bush has <ei148 indicated> the U.S. Navy will <ei149 barricade> the port from Iraqi ships . The president also has <ei150 offered> to <ei151 help> <ei152 offset> Jordan 's costs because 40 percent of its exports <ei154 go> to Iraq and 90 percent of its oil <ei155 comes> from there . " It 's our only outlet to the sea and the rest of the world , " Hussein <ei156 said> . He also <ei157 said> of trade with Iraq : " There are no shipments at the moment . " A day earlier , scores of trucks , many with Iraqi license plates , <ei159 streamed> north out of Aqaba to Amman and onto the desert highway bound for Iraq . The Jordanian monarch <ei160 met> this week with Saddam , but he <ei162 told> reporters he had no message from Baghdad . " I am not <ei163 talking> on behalf of anyone in the area ... but myself , " Hussein <ei164 said> . In the United Nations , Libya <ei165 called> for the <ei166 replacement> of U.S. forces in the Persian Gulf with Arab League forces and U.N. soldiers . Libyan leader Moammar Gadhafi , in a letter to the U.N. Secretary - General , also <ei167 called> for an emergency Security Council <ei168 meeting> in Geneva to <ei169 remove> U.S. forces . There was no <ei170 decision> on a <ei171 meeting> . Thirty-two of the 159 U.N. members had <ei172 filed> compliance reports by Wednesday , and all were <ei174 honoring> the sanctions Iraq . Also Thursday , Saudi Arabia <ei176 called> for an emergency <ei177 conference> of the Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries to <ei178 discuss> how much oil to <ei179 pump> . The minister <ei180 denied> the kingdom had notified notified any of its customers of any <ei181 cutbacks> in oil supply . Reports attributed to the Japanese foreign ministry <ei182 said> Saudi Arabia <ei183 told> U.S., European and Japanese oil companies of a 15-20 percent <ei184 cutback> in its oil supply in September . Meanwhile , Egypt 's official Middle East News Agency <ei186 said> Thursday that Saddam was the <ei188 target> of an assassination <ei189 attempt> , which <ei190 led> to " large-scale " <ei191 arrests> , including some close associates of the Iraqi strongman . The agency <ei192 quoted> witnesses as <ei193 saying> tanks and armored cars are <ei194 patrolling> the streets of Baghdad . There was no independent <ei195 confirmation> of the report by the government-run news agency , which did not <ei196 say> when the reported <ei197 attempt> <ei198 occurred> .

