because he 's <ei214 afraid> we 'll <ei215 find> him , or that internal enemies will , " <ei216 says> one U.S. official . The <ei217 unpredictability> of Iraq 's leader is a principal reason the U.S. is <ei219 going> to such great lengths to build a mammoth force in and around Saudi Arabia . Pentagon officials <ei220 say> the goal is to <ei221 put> 40,000 troops in the region by the end of the month . But the administration is n't <ei223 putting> any upper limit on how high the force could <ei224 go> after that , <ei225 calculating> that it would be a <ei226 mistake> to <ei227 underestimate> and an advantage to <ei228 keep> Saddam Hussein <ei229 guessing> . U.S. commanders in charge of <ei230 planning> for Middle East <ei231 crises> have <ei232 indicated> in the past that they were <ei234 capable> of <ei235 deploying> as many as 300,000 troops . And the U.S. is <ei236 taking> similar steps to <ei237 ensure> that its naval force is <ei238 adequate> to <ei239 carry> out a <ei240 blockade> of Iraq and <ei241 support> a <ei242 war> if necessary . Over the weekend , Pentagon officials <ei244 confirmed> reports that a fourth U.S. aircraft carrier -- the John F. Kennedy -- and its powerful group of support ships could <ei245 head> for the Middle East within a few days . Three other carriers and their escort vessels already are <ei247 stationed> within striking distance of Iraq or are <ei248 steaming> toward the area . But unless the military situation <ei249 changes> drastically , military officials <ei250 say> , the most likely plan will be for the Kennedy to eventually <ei251 replace> the carrier Dwight D. Eisenhower , which has been on patrol since March and was <ei253 scheduled> to <ei254 return> to port before hostilities <ei255 erupted> in Kuwait . -- Andy Pasztor contributed to this article .

