Iraq 's Saddam Hussein , his options for <ei1 ending> the Persian Gulf <ei2 crisis> growing increasingly unpleasant , <ei3 assumed> the <ei4 role> of embattled Arab hero in <ei5 offering> his first rough <ei6 proposal> for a negotiated <ei7 end> to the <ei8 confrontation> . The Iraqi leader , in an " <ei9 initiative> " <ei10 designed> as much to <ei11 rally> Arab public <ei12 opinion> as to <ei13 launch> meaningful <ei14 negotiations> , <ei15 announced> yesterday that he will <ei17 withdraw> his troops from Kuwait only if Israel <ei18 withdraws> from the West Bank and Syria from Lebanon . He apparently <ei19 hopes> to <ei20 lure> <ei21 support> from Arabs who have <ei22 spurned> him so far by <ei23 suggesting> that Iraq will <ei24 use> its <ei25 occupation> of Kuwait as a lever to <ei26 solve> the Arab world 's most frustrating <ei27 problem> , the 23-year Israeli <ei29 occupation> of land <ei30 claimed> by Palestinians . Even as Saddam Hussein was <ei31 searching> for a <ei32 ploy> to <ei33 ease> his isolation , though , the international <ei34 pressure> against him <ei35 clicked> up another notch . The White House yesterday <ei37 disclosed> that Kuwait 's ousted government has formally <ei38 asked> the U.S. to <ei39 enforce> the total trade <ei40 embargo> the United Nations has <ei41 imposed> on Iraq , <ei42 allowing> the U.S. and other nations to immediately <ei43 begin> <ei44 stopping> ships carrying Iraqi goods . Secretary of State James Baker , <ei45 speaking> on ABC News ' " This Week , " <ei46 said> the Kuwaiti <ei47 request> <ei48 gives> the U.S. and other countries " a legal basis for <ei49 stopping> the <ei50 export> of oil and that sort of thing . " The U.S. <ei51 maintains> that under the U.N. charter , the Kuwaiti <ei52 request> <ei53 triggers> <ei54 steps> for the collective <ei55 enforcement> of international <ei56 sanctions> . Mr. Baker <ei57 declined> to <ei58 use> the word blockade , but <ei59 said> that " <ei60 interdiction> " of Iraqi <ei61 shipments> would <ei62 begin> " almost instantly . " In a <ei63 statement> , the White House <ei64 said> it would <ei65 do> " whatever is necessary " to <ei66 ensure> <ei67 compliance> with the <ei68 sanctions> . Other Bush administration officials <ei69 said> that the international naval force in the area -- consisting of American , British , French , Canadian , Soviet , German and Australian ships -- may be <ei70 used> both to <ei71 stop> oil exports from <ei72 leaving> Iraq and Kuwait and to <ei73 stop> shipments of food and other goods from <ei74 going> in. President Bush <ei75 implied> as much yesterday when reporters <ei77 asked> whether the <ei78 interdiction> would <ei79 apply> to food . The president <ei80 responded> , " Everything , everything . " While <ei81 shying> away from actually <ei82 using> the word " blockade , " Mr. Bush <ei83 acknowledged> that the U.S. and others were <ei84 trying> to <ei85 block> <ei86 shipping> to Iraq . " No point getting into all these semantics , " he <ei87 said> . " The main thing is to <ei88 stop> the oil from <ei89 coming> out of there . " The naval interdiction force is part of an overall American strategy that officials say is designed to leave the Iraqi leader with only the stark choice of backing out of Kuwait or launching new attacks to change his situation . Though they insist they are n't trying to lure Saddam Hussein into an attack , officials hope that if he strikes again , the U.S. and its allies will have such an impressive force in place in Saudi Arabia that they will be able to crush him in retaliation . Iraq 's first option , of course , may be simply to sit tight and hope it can endure a trade embargo longer than the West can live without Iraqi and Kuwaiti oil . <ei90 Speaking> on the ABC program , Abdul Amir al-Anbari , Iraq 's ambassador to the U.N., <ei91 asserted> that an <ei92 embargo> on Iraq could <ei93 plunge> the U.S. into a " <ei94 depression> " and the rest of the world into an economic " <ei95 crisis> . " Iraq clearly is <ei96 trying> to <ei97 woo> back more Arab <ei98 support> in case the <ei99 conflict> <ei100 drags> on , <ei101 hoping> that its neighbors eventually will <ei102 help> it <ei103 survive> a prolonged <ei104 war> of economic attrition with the West . So Saddam Hussein on Friday <ei107 tried> to <ei108 scare> other Arab leaders into <ei109 supporting> him by <ei110 calling> on Arabs to <ei111 rise> up in a holy <ei112 war> against leaders who <ei113 invited> American and other Western soldiers into Saudi Arabia to <ei114 protect> the oil-rich kingdom . Then yesterday , he <ei116 tried> to <ei117 entice> Arab leaders with his <ei118 proposal> for a diplomatic solution <ei119 linking> his <ei120 occupation> of Kuwait with Israel 's <ei121 occupation> of the West Bank . The <ei122 proposal> also <ei123 called> for <ei124 replacing> American and other Western troops in Saudi Arabia with Arab forces . The Bush administration immediately <ei125 said> it " categorically " <ei126 rejects> the <ei127 proposals> . And President Bush yesterday , <ei129 asked> whether he was at least glad Iraq is <ei130 discussing> <ei131 negotiations> , <ei132 replied> : " I do n't <ei133 see> anything to be <ei134 pleasing> in there at all . " American strategists are calculating , though , that the trade <ei135 sanctions> -- <ei136 enforced> by an effective though perhaps undeclared naval <ei137 blockade> -- will hold tightly enough to convince Iraq that it will lose in the long run by simply standing pat . At that point , rather than <ei138 go> through the humiliation of <ei139 backing> out of Kuwait , the Iraqis might well <ei140 conclude> that they <ei141 need> to <ei142 lash> out in some way to <ei143 shake> things up . In that event , Saddam Hussein <ei144 appears> to <ei145 have> three choices . The first would be to <ei146 launch> the much-feared direct <ei147 invasion> of Saudi Arabia , <ei148 hoping> to <ei149 seize> some Saudi oil fields and <ei150 improve> his bargaining position . But that option is <ei151 growing> less and less likely as thousands of American , British , Egyptian , Syrian and Moroccan forces <ei153 assemble> in and around Saudi Arabia to <ei154 protect> the kingdom . The Saudis even have in their possession 48 Kuwaiti jet fighters , virtually the entire Kuwaiti air force , which <ei155 managed> to <ei156 escape> the Iraqi <ei157 invasion> , Saudi officials <ei158 said> . The Saudi " window of vulnerability ... is <ei159 closing> very fast , " Prince Bandar bin Sultan , the Saudi ambassador to Washington , <ei160 said> over the weekend . The second possibility would be to <ei162 start> a <ei163 fight> with Israel , in hopes that all Arabs would have to <ei164 move> behind Iraq in a <ei165 fight> against their common Israeli enemy . In such an event , Saddam Hussein also might <ei166 calculate> , the Saudis would be under pressure to <ei167 kick> out U.S. troops because of America 's close ties with Israel . Iraq could <ei168 start> <ei169 hostilities> with Israel either through a direct <ei170 attack> or by <ei171 attacking> Jordan . Israel has publicly <ei172 declared> that it will <ei173 respond> to an Iraqi <ei174 attack> on Jordan because it wo n't <ei176 allow> Iraq 's dangerous army to <ei177 take> control of Jordan 's long border with Israel . Iraq 's third attack option would be to <ei178 start> an undeclared <ei179 war> on the U.S. and other Western nations through terrorism . Two Middle East terrorists with records of successful attacks against Western targets , Abu Nidal and Abu Abbas , have ties to Baghdad . And even terrorist groups that opposed Iraq in its war with Iran show signs of swinging behind Saddam Hussein now that he is in a confrontation with the U.S. And Iraq still <ei181 has> thousands of Americans and other Westerners under its control in Iraq and Kuwait . They are n't being <ei182 allowed> to <ei183 leave> and could <ei184 become> hostages . If Iraq chooses a simple war of nerves and economic attrition , the Bush administration knows a long stalemate could try the patience of the American public and the West in general , and could open the possibility that moderate Arabs -- even including Saudi Arabia -- might drop out of the effort against Iraq and accept some deal from Saddam Hussein . But U.S. officials have <ei185 sized> up Saddam Hussein as a man who , despite some recklessness , will <ei186 back> down if he must . " This is a guy who is impulsive , and therefore capable of big miscalculations , " <ei187 says> one senior administration official involved in <ei188 managing> the <ei189 crisis> . The official <ei190 adds> , though , that " at the same time , we <ei191 think> he is someone who is <ei192 capable> of rational judgments when it comes to power . And when he <ei193 finds> something is unprofitable , then one can <ei194 see> certain <ei195 accommodations> . " Thus , administration aides will be <ei196 trying> to <ei197 calculate> whether Saddam Hussein 's proposed diplomatic formula for <ei198 getting> out of Kuwait <ei199 represents> the first sign he is <ei200 searching> for a way out or simply is a public relations <ei201 stunt> . There are disagreements among experts about how much <ei202 pressure> will be needed to <ei203 make> Saddam Hussein <ei204 decide> he 's up against the wall and whether simple economic pressure will ever be enough . The biggest worry is that if he decides he needs a way out of his predicament but does n't see a face-saving method , he could lash out in dangerous and unpredictable ways . U.S. officials claim they already see signs Saddam Hussein is getting nervous . In the first days after President Bush <ei205 announced> the <ei206 dispatching> of U.S. troops , they note , the Iraqi leader <ei207 made> several nationwide <ei208 addresses> indirectly -- having them <ei209 read> by a television announcer . " That <ei210 shows> he 's <ei211 nervous> about <ei212 pinpointing> his location , either because he 's <ei214 afraid> we 'll <ei215 find> him , or that internal enemies will , " <ei216 says> one U.S. official . The <ei217 unpredictability> of Iraq 's leader is a principal reason the U.S. is <ei219 going> to such great lengths to build a mammoth force in and around Saudi Arabia . Pentagon officials <ei220 say> the goal is to <ei221 put> 40,000 troops in the region by the end of the month . But the administration is n't <ei223 putting> any upper limit on how high the force could <ei224 go> after that , <ei225 calculating> that it would be a <ei226 mistake> to <ei227 underestimate> and an advantage to <ei228 keep> Saddam Hussein <ei229 guessing> . U.S. commanders in charge of <ei230 planning> for Middle East <ei231 crises> have <ei232 indicated> in the past that they were <ei234 capable> of <ei235 deploying> as many as 300,000 troops . And the U.S. is <ei236 taking> similar steps to <ei237 ensure> that its naval force is <ei238 adequate> to <ei239 carry> out a <ei240 blockade> of Iraq and <ei241 support> a <ei242 war> if necessary . Over the weekend , Pentagon officials <ei244 confirmed> reports that a fourth U.S. aircraft carrier -- the John F. Kennedy -- and its powerful group of support ships could <ei245 head> for the Middle East within a few days . Three other carriers and their escort vessels already are <ei247 stationed> within striking distance of Iraq or are <ei248 steaming> toward the area . But unless the military situation <ei249 changes> drastically , military officials <ei250 say> , the most likely plan will be for the Kennedy to eventually <ei251 replace> the carrier Dwight D. Eisenhower , which has been on patrol since March and was <ei253 scheduled> to <ei254 return> to port before hostilities <ei255 erupted> in Kuwait . -- Andy Pasztor contributed to this article .

